SUMMARY:Arbor Evenings
DESCRIPTION:Arbor Evenings 2026\nJune 10 – September 17\, 2026\nBaker Event Lawn\nSip and stroll through the gardens in the glow of the golden hour\, all while listening to live music from a variety of local and regional artists! Arbor Evenings runs Wednesdays and Thursdays June 10 through September 17\, 2026 from 5:30 to 8:30 p.m. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n**Please note: Outside alcohol is prohibited on the campus of The North Carolina Arboretum.** \nThere is no additional cost to attend ArborEvenings beyond our standard parking fee. As always\, Arboretum Society members and their accompanying guests can enter for FREE (guests must be in member vehicles to receive free entry). Proceeds from Arbor Evenings help support The North Carolina Arboretum Society and further advance the Arboretum’s mission. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n5:30 – 8:30: Nicole Kestler Duo (Lively jazz standards)\nEnjoy dinner from a rotating selecting of food trucks!\nScheduled performances are subject to change.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nView upcoming dates here. \nSupport for this event series is generously provided by:
URL:https://ncarboretum.org/event/arbor-evenings-38/
LOCATION:Baker Event Lawn
CATEGORIES:Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://ncarboretum.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Stephan-Pruitt-Photography-North-Carolina-Arboretum-Arbor-Evenings-September-Asheville-81.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Unca": "https://www.unca.edu/event/appalachian-flooding-in-fact-and-fiction/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:unca
X-SOURCE:UNC Asheville
DTSTART;TZID=America/Halifax:20260916T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Halifax:20260916T190000
DTSTAMP:20260803T145147Z
CREATED:20260727T155215Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260803T145147Z
UID:10000114-1789579800-1789585200@www.unca.edu
SUMMARY:Appalachian Flooding in Fact and Fiction
DESCRIPTION:In “Appalachian Flooding in Fact and Fiction\,” Nico Zegre\, Ph.D.\, explores how floods work\, what flooding looks like in Appalachia\, and why floods are changing. He will discuss how the region’s steep topography\, narrow valleys\, and limited flat land for development contribute to significant flood risks\, as well as how flooding connects Appalachian communities through space and time. \nLiterary scholar Jessica Cory\, Ph.D.\, examines representations of flooding in Appalachian literature\, from early works to contemporary depictions in twenty-first-century texts\, including the recent anthologies Had I a Dove: Appalachian Poets on the Helene Flood\, edited by Hilda Downer\, and Troublesome Rising: A Thousand-Year Flood in Eastern Kentucky\, edited by Melissa Helton. Through mapping literary floods across the region\, Cory explores how water connects us\, even in disastrous ways. \nThis event is free and open to the public; however\, registration is encouraged. Register Here \n  \n“Appalachian Flooding in Fact and Fiction” is the third event in the “Rising Waters: Writing Place and Environment” Thomas Howerton Professorship lecture series presented by the UNC Asheville English Department. This three-year series features humanities scholars in conversation with natural sciences scholars about issues affecting us all. \nJessica Cory\nJessica Cory\, Ph.D.\, is a settler scholar and the editor of Appalachian Journal: A Regional Studies Review\, published since 1972 at Appalachian State University. She holds a PhD in Native American\, African American\, and environmental literatures from the University of North Carolina at Greensboro and is also the editor of Mountains Piled upon Mountains: Appalachian Nature Writing in the Anthropocene (WVU Press\, 2019) and the co-editor (with Laura Wright) of Appalachian Ecocriticism and the Paradox of Place (UGA Press\, 2023). Her chapbook\, Disposable\, was published in 2026 with Sheila-Na-Gig editions. Cory’s scholarship and creative writing have appeared in the North Carolina Literary Review\, ISLE\, North Dakota Quarterly\, Still: The Journal\, and many other venues. Born and raised in the foothills of southern Ohio\, she now calls western North Carolina home. \nNico Zegre\nNicolas Zegre\, Ph. D.\, is a professor of forest hydrology and director of the Mountain Hydrology Lab and River Conservation & Management Program at West Virginia University. His research and teaching focus broadly on freshwater security at the intersection of landscape\, people\, and climate in the Appalachian Mountains. He is committed to developing meaningful\, accessible\, and actionable knowledge\, data\, tools\, and applications that inform adaptation\, enhance public discourse\, and promulgate policy. Zegre uses tools such as field measurements\, experiments\, models\, and technology to explore how watersheds work. Likewise\, he uses participatory action and community-engaged research approaches that aim to expand participation in knowledge generation and resilience planning. He sees our world through the lens of water shaped by a lifetime of river exploration. Zegre holds a PhD in forest engineering with a focus on hydrology from Oregon State University. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    Accessibility\nUNC Asheville is committed to providing universal access to all of our events. If you have any questions about access or would like to request reasonable accommodations that will facilitate your full participation in the film screenings\, please contact Stephanie Bullick\, Director of Accessibility\, at accessibility@unca.edu.
URL:https://www.unca.edu/event/appalachian-flooding-in-fact-and-fiction/
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://www.unca.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/UPDATED-Event-Calendar-Graphic-Appalachian-Flooding-in-Fact-and-Fiction.png
END:VEVENT
